WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.280
 Data journalism is a collaborative field with journalists growing into better reporters

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.920
 with the help of practice and partnership.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:19.760
 In this episode of the IRE radio podcast, veteran reporter, editor, and computer programmer

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.200
 Ben Welsh joins to talk about his journalism career and how he got to where he is today.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.880
 As the founder of Reuters News Applications Desk, where he is currently an editor, Welsh

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.720
 has gained a multitude of tips and tricks for data journalism that he will share with

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.800
 you throughout this episode.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:43.280
 Additionally, we discuss his personal site that archives his various data in investigative

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.880
 journalism panels and resources.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:01:05.200
 This is Nakyla Carter and you're listening to IRE radio.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.640
 So the first thing I want to say is just when did you know journalism was your thing and

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.680
 then how did you nurture it to be the person you are today?

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.160
 Oh man, we can go way back with this, I'm getting old.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.880
 I never set out to be a journalist.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.280
 It wasn't something I did much in high school or a career goal I went into college with.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.800
 I was an undergraduate at DePaul University in Chicago, Illinois after growing up in Eastern

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.760
 Iowa and kind of stumbled into journalism accidentally.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.880
 I had a job answering phones in the communication department as part of kind of one of these

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:43.320
 work study programs to make a little money while I was in school and that kind of thing.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.160
 In that job I was entrusted with some very important responsibilities.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.820
 One of them was pinning up flyers on the bulletin boards around campus about upcoming events

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.360
 that the department was running.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.320
 So I was often the first person to see the notice of new things happening.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.200
 One day I was pinning up the flyer and I noticed that it was an advertisement for an internship

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.200
 with two journalists who were taking up residency at the university, Carol Marine and Don Mosley.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.780
 They were looking for a student to join them.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.240
 I was pinning in, I thought, "Hey, that sounds cool."

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.620
 It sounds a lot cooler than this job pinning up flyers on bulletin boards.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.960
 Because I was the first person to see the flyer, I suspect I was the first person to

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:24.960
 apply.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.600
 I never asked how many other people applied, but I ended up getting that internship despite

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:36.680
 being ridiculously unqualified and began my journalism career, as it was, as the grunt

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:42.380
 assistant to this producer and correspondent team who were doing weekly television pieces

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.080
 and newspaper columns and some long-form documentaries.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.560
 I got into journalism as an accident and pretty quickly saw it was a lot of fun.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.840
 It was a great way to engage with the world and learn about it and was also a challenge

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.400
 and maybe something I could do.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.120
 I think that was amazing because I think it's important to understand that everybody's

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.360
 plight into journalism is completely different.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.360
 It's always interesting to hear how did you get started there.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.280
 You talk about how you kind of got into the journalism realm.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.880
 How did you start doing more so data type of things?

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.760
 I was, as I said, pretty unqualified to work in this journalism office I was working in,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:27.720
 but data and the work that I do now today quickly found me in a public records request.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.960
 We got a tip that a local suburb of the city had.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:38.440
 The tip was that a local alderman had been bid-rigging some legal contracts in this city

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.280
 to rack up some money for himself and his allies.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.640
 To check it out, we filed a public records request asking for a list of every legal contract

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.760
 or fee that was paid out to the city, to this particular law firm.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.120
 It was my first FOIA ever.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.940
 After a few weeks or months, I can't remember exactly, finally in the mail came a big long

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.560
 printout, a computer printout.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.640
 This is like a 1990s or '80s style one where it's an accordion sheet of paper, dozens of

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.080
 pages connected by the little tear strips on the outside.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.640
 It was all the legal fees, but they were spread out in this dot matrix style across a lot

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.040
 of paper.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.920
 We initially had the question, "Well, how the heck are we going to make sense of this?"

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:24.200
 I honestly don't remember or don't know where I got the idea, but I thought, "Hey, what

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.880
 if I type it all into a spreadsheet?"

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:27.880
 It kind of came to me.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:28.880
 I sat down there.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.920
 It took me a day or two to go through it all and double and triple check it.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.320
 We arrived at kind of a pretty big number, a big enough number to justify the story being

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.320
 on the news.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.960
 We kind of got our little scoop, our investigative piece, out of the tip, the FOIA, and the data

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.200
 entry.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.240
 With that, I kind of was hooked, one, I saw the data was kind of a shortcut to getting

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.120
 to work on really cool stories, more than just kind of the run of the mill stuff, things

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.280
 I found exciting.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.760
 Hey, as the guy who could sit down and figure out the tech, it might be a niche for me or

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.520
 a place for me to be the guy who fills that gap and helps make those stories happen.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.200
 Those two things kind of got me going on it.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.000
 I wanted to go further down that road, so I went to the University of Missouri and was

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.060
 a graduate assistant at IRE NYCAR, where I really got to focus on not just faking it,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.560
 but actually learning how to do these skills, learning how to code, learning what real analysis

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.040
 was, getting a lot more practice.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.240
 That set me off onto the path I still am today, now more than 20 years later.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.440
 On that respect, I think that you'd grow so much as a data journalist by obviously knowing

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:39.120
 the practical experience, but also talking to people and collaboration and learning other

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.120
 people's tips and tricks.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.280
 So I have to talk about your website, PowerWire, because I was like, this is amazing.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.320
 I wish I had seen this last year.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.060
 But yeah, like what made you want to compile all that information?

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.240
 Because it's not something that you're required to do, but it's amazingly helpful.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.440
 Well, you know, it's nothing that happened overnight.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.400
 It's the accumulation of kind of years and years of chipping stuff together.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.920
 But you know, for me, the annual IRE NYCAR conference is really just kind of an opportunity

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.560
 to challenge myself and for all of us in our community to come together, to say, what do

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.800
 we want to teach each other?

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:12.800
 What's important?

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.020
 What have we learned in the last year that's worth passing on to other people or that we're

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.960
 excited about or that we want to share?

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.640
 You know, and I look at every year's conference as kind of that type of moment or opportunity

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.880
 and try to drum up new stuff.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.240
 And I've, you know, been doing it long enough that I kind of got a little playbook for it,

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.160
 a little routine, and I've just been adding, you know, incrementally more and more of these

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.120
 kind of lessons that I put together with other people who helped me, by the way, of course.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.120
 And it's become, you know, over time, this collection.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.640
 And do you have any favorites or any talks that you have that, you know, you look forward

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.640
 to saying?

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.320
 Because I know some of them you repeat and some of them you tweak and things like that.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:55.380
 But is there ever one where you kind of are like, I wish that something like this existed

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.840
 when I first got started?

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.240
 Well, I don't know if I have a favorite child, so to speak.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.080
 To me, the best thing about it for me, there's a lot of great things about it.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:08.200
 I love coming together with peers and people I respect to help create the classes and that

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:14.320
 sort of formative experience of us collaborating to really take an idea or a technique that

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.760
 hasn't really been fully fleshed out and trying to put it on paper and get it across.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.020
 Like, that creative process is really stimulating and always rewarding to me, and that keeps

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.020
 me doing it.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.040
 And then, you know, the conference itself is a great experience, but, you know, maybe most

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.320
 of all is the thing that comes out of putting it on the web and just kind of having a little

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:39.020
 bit of faith in the world or giving it out there is I am just often stunned to hear from

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.360
 people on the other side of the planet who I've never met, never will, who will reach

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.920
 out and say they learned this or that just based on a resource that we put on the web,

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:49.640
 not really knowing what would happen with it.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:56.320
 And there's something that is kind of magical to that, that is a hit I keep chasing or that

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.120
 I just love, and it really reminds me of my own formative experiences on the internet

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.720
 as a teenager in the 90s, you know, I'm old enough that, you know, I remember life before

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.760
 the web and as a kid in rural Iowa, its arrival was a big deal.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:15.720
 And the internet was really my kind of gateway to the world and such an exciting, stimulating

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:21.480
 place to learn and connect and to still have experiences like that 30 years later with people

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:28.080
 in Ukraine or Brazil or wherever else is just, it's the magic of technology that I find so

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:29.080
 rewarding.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:33.840
 You know, I'm learning about your plight to where you are today and you went from not

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.160
 knowing that journalism was something that you even were interested in to creating an

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.720
 entire news applications desk.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:45.600
 How did you know that that is something that that newsroom needed and how do you operate

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.320
 in that position?

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:53.680
 Not sounding too grandiose or self-assured, I think we are entering kind of a new era

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.800
 of data journalism in kind of the commercial space, commercial media, which is I think

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.080
 one of industrialization and specialization.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.920
 You know, when I started out at the LA Times in 2007, the whole idea of putting data on

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.400
 the internet on a newspaper website was still a pretty new one.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.820
 We weren't the first to do it for sure, you know what I mean?

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:18.040
 But in our context, it was definitely new, and I think a lot of what was going on even

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.600
 at the leading news organizations then was really experimental.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.760
 You had really small teams of people kind of goofing around trying to make things happen

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.480
 on the web, often totally separate from their mainline newsrooms.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.240
 You know, it's good to remember that it was that 2008 election was probably really the

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.880
 first one where you had really widespread live election results on newspaper and news

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.720
 organization websites.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.880
 When Obama was first elected, they did, there was examples of that before, but the really

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.120
 kind of first like widespread publication of it was that time.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.440
 And it was really kind of an experiment that was then wildly successful.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:59.100
 And I think there's been many other examples since then running up to coronavirus trackers.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:03.520
 And you can go down the list of all these different ways that taking data directly to

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:09.560
 the audience in the form of dashboards and databases and visualizations that aren't traditional

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:10.880
 stories, you know what I mean?

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.240
 That has gone from an experiment to really a widget or a product that we want to manufacture,

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:22.000
 you know, inside the metaphorical assembly line or factory of your newsroom.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.260
 And so it's my view as we enter, as we want to go from experiment to mass production,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:34.480
 we need to, you know, institutionalize, specialize, and really ramp up how we are organized to

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.120
 create these widgets, you know?

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.480
 And in my view, that means making a news desk, which is its own little assembly line in the

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:48.480
 factory that focuses not on the kind of battleship investigative reporting projects, not necessarily

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:56.720
 on kind of one-off graphics to accompany traditional text stories, but instead on these data products,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.400
 whatever you want to call them, that are incredibly popular with audiences and are also, it could

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.300
 be Capital J journalism as well, right?

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:10.000
 And so this news application's desk is our attempt to name something that does that.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.040
 I don't know if I love the name, if you have a better name, I'd love to hear it, but that's

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.040
 what we mean.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:19.580
 Basically, that's what we're trying to do here at Reuters, is to create a group of people

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.680
 who just focus on making that type of thing.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:26.000
 I think that's amazing, especially because, like you said, we're moving towards a time

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.960
 in data journalism where I like to say that people almost expect data to be in the story.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.240
 That's become way more normal, and so I think it's amazing that you guys are thinking about

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:37.240
 this.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.640
 Yeah, I think we can't forget that.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:43.520
 I think because data journalism grew out of the investigative reporting tradition, or at

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.560
 least a large part of it did, there's many streams and strands, there's not one way.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:52.240
 But the IRE/NICAR tradition is really one that stems back to investigative reporting,

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.760
 which is really what I was trained in and what I love and respect, and I've done a lot of

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:01.720
 it in my career, but the justification for that type of work, for the investment, for

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:08.760
 the dollars to be spent to fund it, is typically one that's based on prestige and public service,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:14.160
 which are two of the very high call-ins of our profession, but in a time of decreasing

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.680
 resources and brutal disruption to the economic model that supports journalism, it can be

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.040
 difficult to justify jobs and investment in data journalism if it's strictly seen as a

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.080
 prestige project, so that's bad news, baby.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:32.400
 But the good news is, and I think we often undersell this good news, is that these data

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.640
 products I'm talking about, your live election results or your coronavirus tracker, these

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.960
 are, yes, they are public service, and yes, they ought to be seen as prestigious.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.320
 They have won some of the highest awards, but they are also popular, right?

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.000
 I think last year, Reuters.com, the most popular thing all year, were the live election results

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:57.500
 done by our team in London, led by John McClure, and the most popular thing in the history

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.440
 of the Los Angeles Times, where I used to work for the most read piece of journalism

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:06.640
 in the 100-and-whatever-year history of the LA Times, was its coronavirus tracker, and

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:11.680
 I suspect the same is for the New York Times and many, many other news organizations, and

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.120
 I think we shouldn't sell ourselves short.

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:17.240
 We're making things people want.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:22.720
 A lot of people say journalists and math don't go together, or a lot of journalists aren't,

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:26.960
 I guess, set up naturally to do data, stuff like that, but what is your advice for someone

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.640
 who wants to get into that realm of things?

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.320
 It is a hot commodity right now, and it's an amazing skill set to have.

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:35.320
 Well, then, that's your advantage.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:36.320
 You know what I mean?

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.440
 If other people are avoiding it and you're willing to take it on, that's going to give

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:45.520
 you the edge, that's going to give you the front foot, and I think you should not listen

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.800
 to people who are discouraging you to not go down this road if it's where you want to

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:53.920
 go, because, as you just said, we know it's where things are headed, and we know that

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:59.560
 it's valuable, and so don't be afraid to have the confidence of that and surround yourself

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:05.320
 with people who agree with you and find mentors and role models that'll help you move down

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.320
 that path.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.040
 It's a lane that's wide open.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.480
 What are some things that you live by when you're doing data analysis and news application

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.640
 creation?

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.080
 I don't know if I can rewind.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:17.720
 What would I tell younger me?

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:21.880
 I mean, I've studied harder in high school, you know what I mean?

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:23.480
 I don't know.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:27.680
 I don't have any regrets or any big thing like that.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:31.720
 There's reassurance, I think, we all need when we're young and that I needed like everyone

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:35.240
 else, which is if you work hard, it will pay off.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:39.280
 I've seen that in my own life, and I've seen it in many others, and I would just encourage

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.960
 people to ... I almost said lean in.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:42.960
 I won't say lean in.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.040
 I'll say work hard and be a good person and it'll work out.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.400
 That's what I would tell myself because I was a very nervous, young, country kid in

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:52.400
 the city.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:58.640
 As far as the work we're doing, I mean, to me, you got to have high standards for yourself

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:02.840
 and you can't cut corners and you got to do the work.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:07.520
 I think that that means coming in every day like you got something to prove, not being

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:12.840
 afraid of challenges and realizing that by pushing through them is how you're going to

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.840
 do something you're proud of.

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:15.840
 Don't be lazy.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:18.200
 It's the same message maybe, but it's really what I believe.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:23.120
 I think it's all about progress and push and not giving up.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:24.960
 I have to ask some things.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.880
 Do you prefer Python or R?

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:27.880
 Why?

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:33.800
 I'm definitely a Python person, but I respect R and really about the results.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:38.680
 I think there's a lot of great and talented people who work in R, but Python is obviously

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:39.680
 superior.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:40.680
 I agree.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.160
 I wholeheartedly agree.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:46.560
 And then I wanted to ask, what is your favorite data journalism memory?

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.320
 Something that if it's a story or maybe an interview or just something that happened

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.280
 where you're just like, yeah, this is the career for me.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:56.000
 Well, there's that first story I told you earlier where I kind of had the spark of the

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:57.240
 light bulb went on.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.000
 This could be something for me.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:04.160
 To me, the most powerful moments are often, and maybe it does go back to those investigative

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:09.440
 pieces, but when statistics and data lead me to someone's doorstep or to somewhere in

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:15.160
 the world and help both me, the journalist who's trying to figure out the story, but

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:20.400
 more importantly in the moment and maybe in the cosmic sense, the people who lived the

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:25.960
 story helped them better understand what the heck happened to them in life, particularly

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.960
 if it was something bad.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:33.560
 I did a whole series of stories about the local 911 system in Los Angeles where we uncovered

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:38.560
 a lot of systemic flaws and how emergency care is delivered, how the fire department

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:39.800
 is run.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.320
 And some of the most powerful memories they had is where the statistics would tell us,

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:44.960
 well, there's this kink in the system.

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:48.520
 There's this inefficiency, there's this problem, and it could be having really bad effects

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.640
 out there in the world.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.480
 But then we followed the data, individual calls out to doors and knocked on them to

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:57.800
 see what really happened in this case or that case where the data says something maybe went

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:58.800
 wrong.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.800
 And what we found often is, yeah, it did go wrong.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:05.640
 And so there's this way in which you kind of discovered a story based on a spreadsheet.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:10.560
 But then also there's this powerful moment of connection with the person on the other

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:17.600
 end of the journalistic exchange where you help them understand why something bad happened

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:22.600
 in their life and help them be part of an effort to describe and share that with their

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:26.760
 community so that maybe it can be fixed and it doesn't happen to someone else.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:31.560
 And to me, those moments of where statistics and shoe leather and the kind of connection

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:37.000
 with a subject come together are kind of these magical moments that data does make possible

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:42.640
 because when you don't just go off your own bias or instincts, when you let the math and

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.680
 science lead the way, you can have these powerful moments of discovery.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:51.040
 And they're not the same as Albert Einstein and E=MC squared or whatever.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.480
 But they are powerful.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:57.360
 And so I struggle to describe it and contextually it would be almost impossible to give you

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:01.240
 every detail of what that was really like without writing a novel or something.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:06.480
 But like those moments of discovery and connection where statistics and shoe leather meat are

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:11.960
 intense.

